1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,746
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,766 --> 00:00:08,740
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:14,302
This is Lecture 29, typical garment factories.

4
00:00:14,702 --> 00:00:16,981
Last time we balanced one line.

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:22,692
Today we visit five kinds of factory and ask why plants that all make clothes

6
00:00:22,712 --> 00:00:24,027
look so different.

7
00:00:25,547 --> 00:00:27,447
Here's the problem.

8
00:00:27,847 --> 00:00:35,381
A T-shirt factory wins a big order for a basic crew-neck tee at 200 an hour.

9
00:00:35,781 --> 00:00:46,716
Its 16 operations total 5.841 minutes: overlock 1.863, coverstitch 1.506,

10
00:00:46,736 --> 00:00:56,921
lockstitch 1.334, hand 1.138, the longest being side seams at 0.655.

11
00:00:57,321 --> 00:01:05,541
The manager plans one operation each, staffed by SMV over takt rounded up.

12
00:01:05,941 --> 00:01:09,222
What's the takt and the minimum headcount?

13
00:01:09,622 --> 00:01:14,202
How many, at what balance, operation by operation?

14
00:01:14,602 --> 00:01:17,782
And merging by machine type?

15
00:01:19,302 --> 00:01:22,562
Same garments, different factories: why?.

16
00:01:24,082 --> 00:01:28,542
Each kind of factory has its signature.

17
00:01:28,942 --> 00:01:34,445
Suits have over 300 operations, and every piece differs.

18
00:01:34,845 --> 00:01:40,505
Shirts spend their time on small parts: collars, cuffs, plackets.

19
00:01:40,905 --> 00:01:45,605
T-shirts are knit, so overlock and coverstitch rule.

20
00:01:46,005 --> 00:01:47,958
Knitwear panels are knitted.

21
00:01:48,358 --> 00:01:54,341
Quick response takes orders of a hundred and lives on fast changeovers.

22
00:01:54,741 --> 00:01:55,982
Why the differences?

23
00:01:56,382 --> 00:02:03,194
Product structure sets the operations, fabric sets the stitches and machines,

24
00:02:03,214 --> 00:02:06,122
batch size sets the organisation.

25
00:02:07,642 --> 00:02:12,858
Made-to-measure suits: data first, then single-piece flow.

26
00:02:14,378 --> 00:02:17,799
What makes suits hard is that every piece differs.

27
00:02:18,199 --> 00:02:23,498
The bundle system ties 10 to 30 same-size pieces together;

28
00:02:23,518 --> 00:02:27,074
once every piece differs, it collapses.

29
00:02:27,474 --> 00:02:30,708
So made-to-measure plants run two rows.

30
00:02:31,108 --> 00:02:37,382
Data on top: a 3D scan in about 7 seconds produces the marker file,

31
00:02:37,402 --> 00:02:41,328
the hanger route and station instructions.

32
00:02:41,728 --> 00:02:47,591
Material below: cut one at a time, hung with an electronic tag,

33
00:02:47,611 --> 00:02:52,447
and the operator's swipe shows what this suit needs.

34
00:02:53,967 --> 00:02:55,467
How do hangers route?

35
00:02:55,867 --> 00:03:01,893
A reader at each spur entrance reads the hanger, looks up its next operation,

36
00:03:01,913 --> 00:03:06,683
and switches it in only if this station does it and has room,

37
00:03:06,703 --> 00:03:08,567
favouring idle stations.

38
00:03:08,967 --> 00:03:10,503
Now sleeve setting:

39
00:03:10,523 --> 00:03:15,664
the cap is 3 centimetres longer than the 47-centimetre armhole,

40
00:03:15,684 --> 00:03:17,548
about 6.4 percent ease,

41
00:03:17,568 --> 00:03:22,627
distributed with plenty at the crown and almost none underarm.

42
00:03:23,027 --> 00:03:30,573
The machine sets upper and lower feeds separately, like differential feed.

43
00:03:32,093 --> 00:03:34,455
Why is single-piece flow fast?

44
00:03:34,855 --> 00:03:40,635
Little's law: throughput time equals WIP over output, for any stable system.

45
00:03:41,035 --> 00:03:52,197
A suit line making 30 an hour with 1500 in progress keeps each suit 50 hours;

46
00:03:52,217 --> 00:03:56,118
cut WIP to 300 and it's 10.

47
00:03:56,518 --> 00:04:06,820
A shirt line growing bundles from 10 to 70 stretched throughput from 0.61 to

48
00:04:06,840 --> 00:04:08,178
4.16 days.

49
00:04:08,578 --> 00:04:10,918
Short lead times need low WIP.

50
00:04:12,438 --> 00:04:16,001
Shirts: the time goes into small parts.

51
00:04:17,521 --> 00:04:18,856
Shirts are different.

52
00:04:19,256 --> 00:04:25,996
Their 41 sewing operations take about 22.3 minutes; where does it go?

53
00:04:26,396 --> 00:04:36,748
Collar 3.62, cuffs 3.85, plackets 2.21: those three are 43 percent,

54
00:04:36,768 --> 00:04:42,786
all parts 66 percent, assembly a third.

55
00:04:43,186 --> 00:04:48,526
So shirt plants run five part lines into one assembly line.

56
00:04:48,926 --> 00:04:54,035
The small parts are flat, fixed in shape and repetitive:

57
00:04:54,055 --> 00:04:58,066
ideal for template-plus-X-Y automatic units.

58
00:04:59,586 --> 00:05:02,166
T-shirts: staffing and balance.

59
00:05:03,686 --> 00:05:08,345
T-shirts are knit: stretchy, curling, running at cut edges,

60
00:05:08,365 --> 00:05:12,469
so seams use four-thread overlock, hems coverstitch,

61
00:05:12,489 --> 00:05:15,641
and lockstitch only labels and bartacks;

62
00:05:15,661 --> 00:05:19,923
overlock and coverstitch carry 58 percent of the work.

63
00:05:20,323 --> 00:05:27,063
Takt is how often the line must deliver: 18 seconds for 200 an hour.

64
00:05:27,463 --> 00:05:37,013
Theoretical headcount is total time over takt, 5.841 over 0.30, about 19.5,

65
00:05:37,033 --> 00:05:38,928
so at least 20.

66
00:05:40,447 --> 00:05:45,798
The chart switches between two plans; bars are load per person,

67
00:05:45,818 --> 00:05:47,248
the line is takt.

68
00:05:47,648 --> 00:05:55,749
In the published 22-person plan the tallest bar is thread trimming with one person,

69
00:05:55,769 --> 00:05:58,489
capping output at about 153;

70
00:05:58,509 --> 00:06:03,577
add one person there and the cap rises to about 174,

71
00:06:03,597 --> 00:06:07,588
anywhere else and almost nothing changes.

72
00:06:07,988 --> 00:06:14,782
Staffing each operation by takt takes 27 people, all bars under takt,

73
00:06:14,802 --> 00:06:17,448
many far below it: waiting.

74
00:06:18,968 --> 00:06:25,828
The lab actually runs the line, with operation times varying by 12 percent.

75
00:06:26,228 --> 00:06:33,607
On the left, bundles: 600 pieces in progress queuing between stations,

76
00:06:33,627 --> 00:06:38,681
so a garment takes about three and a half hours.

77
00:06:39,080 --> 00:06:44,989
On the right, hangers with 21 people grouped by machine,

78
00:06:45,009 --> 00:06:52,294
work sent to idle stations: about 190 an hour, fifty-odd on the line,

79
00:06:52,314 --> 00:06:54,941
under 20 minutes through.

80
00:06:55,340 --> 00:07:00,321
The organisation decides how much paper balance survives.

81
00:07:01,840 --> 00:07:05,100
Knitwear, quick response and choosing equipment.

82
00:07:06,620 --> 00:07:13,068
Knitwear panels are knitted to shape, then linked loop by loop,

83
00:07:13,088 --> 00:07:15,840
about 32 minutes a garment.

84
00:07:16,240 --> 00:07:22,844
Fully-fashioned work takes about 52 labour-minutes, 60 percent linking;

85
00:07:22,864 --> 00:07:29,467
whole-garment knitting cuts that to about 17 but ties up the machine 30

86
00:07:29,487 --> 00:07:30,960
minutes a piece.

87
00:07:31,360 --> 00:07:36,870
For 1000 a day: about 13 machines and 67 linkers,

88
00:07:36,890 --> 00:07:40,821
or about 23 whole-garment machines.

89
00:07:41,221 --> 00:07:44,761
It saves people and costs machine time.

90
00:07:46,281 --> 00:07:50,730
For quick response, changeover costs twice: the stoppage,

91
00:07:50,750 --> 00:07:56,531
re-arranging machines, rethreading, setting and test sewing; and learning,

92
00:07:56,551 --> 00:08:02,960
with one study finding first-day efficiency only about 63 percent of steady state,

93
00:08:02,980 --> 00:08:05,861
recovering over three time constants.

94
00:08:06,261 --> 00:08:08,321
Both are spread over the batch.

95
00:08:08,721 --> 00:08:15,454
The chart cycles the batch from 150 to 3000:

96
00:08:15,474 --> 00:08:26,043
at 150 the long line costs 74 labour-minutes a piece and the cell 28;

97
00:08:26,063 --> 00:08:29,266
they cross near 3828.

98
00:08:30,786 --> 00:08:38,183
A cell has 4 to 17 people, each doing two to four operations, machines in a U,

99
00:08:38,203 --> 00:08:41,606
only the pieces in hand on the line.

100
00:08:42,006 --> 00:08:46,351
The relay zone is clever: nobody owns fixed operations,

101
00:08:46,371 --> 00:08:51,193
each carries a garment forward; the last operator, when done,

102
00:08:51,213 --> 00:08:56,669
takes over from the one before, who walks back to the one before him,

103
00:08:56,689 --> 00:08:59,288
and the first fetches new pieces.

104
00:08:59,688 --> 00:09:04,949
Order them slowest to fastest and the cell balances itself.

105
00:09:06,468 --> 00:09:10,676
On a car body-welding line robots work in series,

106
00:09:10,696 --> 00:09:14,472
and every cell's cycle must stay under takt;

107
00:09:14,492 --> 00:09:18,096
the one that exceeds it is the bottleneck,

108
00:09:18,116 --> 00:09:21,288
the same chart as the T-shirt line's.

109
00:09:21,688 --> 00:09:26,306
There's no half robot, so rounding losses appear too,

110
00:09:26,326 --> 00:09:29,544
and the fix is merging similar tasks.

111
00:09:29,944 --> 00:09:36,522
Warehouse robots dispatched to the shortest queue follow the hanger system's rule,

112
00:09:36,542 --> 00:09:39,981
and tote buffers obey Little's law as well.

113
00:09:41,501 --> 00:09:43,121
You meet Little's law every day.

114
00:09:43,521 --> 00:09:49,299
Thirty people are queuing at the canteen window and it serves two a minute,

115
00:09:49,319 --> 00:09:53,473
so you'll wait about thirty over two, fifteen minutes.

116
00:09:53,873 --> 00:09:59,249
To wait less, either open another window, which raises output,

117
00:09:59,269 --> 00:10:04,733
or keep fewer people in the queue, which cuts work in progress.

118
00:10:05,133 --> 00:10:12,399
A sewing floor shortens lead times by shrinking bundles and WIP for exactly

119
00:10:12,419 --> 00:10:13,953
the same reason.

120
00:10:15,473 --> 00:10:16,972
Back to our problem.

121
00:10:17,372 --> 00:10:27,024
Takt is 0.30 minutes, 18 seconds; theoretical headcount about 19.5,

122
00:10:27,044 --> 00:10:28,756
at least 20.

123
00:10:29,156 --> 00:10:32,785
Rounding each operation up gives 27 people,

124
00:10:32,805 --> 00:10:38,723
bottlenecked at rib joining with one person, about 72 percent balance,

125
00:10:38,743 --> 00:10:41,523
every fraction turned to waiting.

126
00:10:41,923 --> 00:10:49,865
Grouped by machine, 7 overlock, 5 coverstitch, 5 lockstitch and 4 hand,

127
00:10:49,885 --> 00:10:55,023
21 in all at about 92 percent: 6 fewer people.

128
00:10:56,543 --> 00:11:00,488
Now Virtual lab 29-1, simulating a sewing floor,

129
00:11:00,508 --> 00:11:06,188
which you can do yourself on WenQuest. Results and tasks are blurred.

130
00:11:06,208 --> 00:11:11,309
The guide is on the left; on the right is the operation table:

131
00:11:11,329 --> 00:11:17,257
each row has an operation, machine type, standard minutes and headcount;

132
00:11:17,277 --> 00:11:23,204
the bar is load per person and the line is takt. Choose the bundle line,

133
00:11:23,224 --> 00:11:28,243
one operator per operation, and click 'Source configuration'.

134
00:11:28,643 --> 00:11:32,690
Below is the floor plan. Dots are operators, filled when working,

135
00:11:32,710 --> 00:11:36,068
hollow when waiting; yellow blocks are queued bundles.

136
00:11:36,088 --> 00:11:41,198
Fast-forward at 20 minutes a second and look for the red bar in the balance chart.

137
00:11:41,218 --> 00:11:44,577
Task one: add just one person, try several operations,

138
00:11:44,597 --> 00:11:47,767
restart and skip to the end of the shift each time,

139
00:11:47,787 --> 00:11:49,769
and see where output rises most.

140
00:11:50,169 --> 00:11:56,845
Click 'Staff each operation to takt' and record the headcount and balance.

141
00:11:56,865 --> 00:12:00,284
Then switch to 'Pool by machine type',

142
00:12:00,304 --> 00:12:05,260
where one team shares all operations on a machine type.

143
00:12:05,280 --> 00:12:10,056
Task two asks for 182 an hour or more with 21 people.

144
00:12:10,456 --> 00:12:17,538
Task three stays with bundles: tune the bundle size and the WIP cap, restart,

145
00:12:17,558 --> 00:12:23,903
skip to shift end, and cut the throughput time without losing output.

146
00:12:23,923 --> 00:12:30,914
Then check from your records that WIP is about output times throughput time.

147
00:12:31,314 --> 00:12:35,101
Switch to hangers and watch them circle the main rail,

148
00:12:35,121 --> 00:12:38,344
switched into a station only when it has room.

149
00:12:38,364 --> 00:12:42,856
Task four wants high output with little WIP. Then in the U cell,

150
00:12:42,876 --> 00:12:48,002
swap multi-skilled operators with the arrows and compare putting the fast

151
00:12:48,022 --> 00:12:50,540
ones at the entrance or at the exit.

152
00:12:50,940 --> 00:12:55,491
Last, batch size and style changes: move the order size,

153
00:12:55,511 --> 00:13:00,552
the long line's changeover downtime and its learning constant,

154
00:13:00,572 --> 00:13:03,572
and watch where the two curves cross.

155
00:13:03,592 --> 00:13:08,959
Task six asks you to bring the break-even batch under 1000 pieces.

156
00:13:08,979 --> 00:13:13,040
The guide and report template are on the platform.

157
00:13:14,560 --> 00:13:20,247
Everything in this lecture is on the WenQuest learning platform.

158
00:13:20,647 --> 00:13:28,394
You can open the interactive slides and the line simulation and work them yourself,

159
00:13:28,414 --> 00:13:32,044
including the pages this video skipped.

160
00:13:32,444 --> 00:13:38,278
You submit the assignment and the lab report on the platform,

161
00:13:38,298 --> 00:13:41,349
and get marks and comments back.

162
00:13:41,749 --> 00:13:48,129
Questions go to the discussion forum, and the grades page shows every mark.

163
00:13:49,649 --> 00:13:52,652
The assignment has three questions. One:

164
00:13:52,672 --> 00:13:58,170
using this lecture's 16 operation times, raise the target to 250 an hour.

165
00:13:58,190 --> 00:14:01,194
Find takt and the theoretical headcount,

166
00:14:01,214 --> 00:14:05,427
staff the line per operation and pooled by machine type,

167
00:14:05,447 --> 00:14:08,148
give headcount and balance for each,

168
00:14:08,168 --> 00:14:11,549
and explain where the extra people come from.

169
00:14:11,949 --> 00:14:19,210
Two: the line used bundles, with 600 pieces in progress at 175 an hour;

170
00:14:19,230 --> 00:14:23,313
with hangers it holds 55 at 191 an hour.

171
00:14:23,333 --> 00:14:28,133
Use Little's law to find both throughput times,

172
00:14:28,153 --> 00:14:32,030
and explain why hangers keep WIP down.

173
00:14:32,430 --> 00:14:36,157
Three: the factory starts taking 300-piece orders.

174
00:14:36,177 --> 00:14:41,627
Use this lecture's changeover model to find labour per piece for the long

175
00:14:41,647 --> 00:14:43,126
line and the U cell,

176
00:14:43,146 --> 00:14:47,398
recompute with zero changeover downtime on the long line,

177
00:14:47,418 --> 00:14:52,269
and propose your organisation and at least two equipment changes.

178
00:14:52,669 --> 00:14:59,441
Submit the assignment and the lab report on the WenQuest platform.

179
00:15:00,961 --> 00:15:04,563
There's also a ten-question quiz. For example:

180
00:15:04,583 --> 00:15:09,917
a suit line makes 25 suits an hour with 400 in progress. On average,

181
00:15:09,937 --> 00:15:13,460
how many hours does a suit spend on the line?

182
00:15:13,860 --> 00:15:14,247
Or:

183
00:15:14,267 --> 00:15:25,241
a T-shirt line's 16 operations total 5.841 minutes and the target is 180 an hour.

184
00:15:25,261 --> 00:15:29,720
What's the theoretical headcount?

185
00:15:30,120 --> 00:15:36,218
You get three attempts, and the explanations appear as soon as you submit.

186
00:15:37,738 --> 00:15:39,718
Three things to remember.

187
00:15:40,118 --> 00:15:45,965
One: the product sets the operations, the operations the machines,

188
00:15:45,985 --> 00:15:52,098
the batch size the organisation; the five factories follow from that.

189
00:15:52,498 --> 00:15:58,065
Two: Little's law, throughput time equals WIP over output;

190
00:15:58,085 --> 00:16:04,518
single-piece flow and hangers shorten lead time by keeping WIP low.

191
00:16:04,918 --> 00:16:11,243
Three: grouping operations by machine type cuts rounding loss,

192
00:16:11,263 --> 00:16:15,131
lifting balance from 72 to 92 percent;

193
00:16:15,151 --> 00:16:20,043
below the crossover batch the U cell is cheaper.

194
00:16:20,443 --> 00:16:22,866
The slides, the virtual lab,

195
